{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011cYBPTqmrWi65URXi77HUn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Go Enum Type Pattern\n2\t\n3\tThis guide documents the canonical string-based enum pattern used throughout Benjamin Borbe's Go projects - a widely used pattern (50+ implementations across projects).\n4\t\n5\t## When to Use This Pattern\n6\t\n7\tUse this pattern when you need:\n8\t- **Type-safe constants** with validation against a known set of values\n9\t- **Compile-time guarantees** instead of runtime string matching\n10\t- **Collection operations** (Contains, filtering, etc.)\n11\t- **Database/JSON serialization** of enumerated values\n12\t\n13\t**Don't use** for simple value wrappers that don't need predefined constants (e.g., user IDs, arbitrary names).\n14\t\n15\t## Core Pattern Structure\n16\t\n17\t### RULE go-enum-type/typed-constants-with-collection (MUST)\n18\t\n19\t**Owner**: go-architecture-assistant\n20\t**Applies when**: a Go package introduces a finite set of enum-like values (status codes, kinds, modes, etc.) declared as untyped `const` strings or `const` ints, without a paired typed `string`/`int` newtype AND without an `Available<Name>s` collection containing every valid value.\n21\t**Enforcement**: `rules/go/typed-constants-with-collection.yml` (mechanical first-pass flags untyped `const` string specs) + judgment-tier LLM adjudication to confirm shared semantic prefix and check whether the package already has a typed newtype + `Available*` collection.\n22\t**Why**: Untyped enum constants spread through the codebase as bare strings — every call site can pass any string, every comparison can typo, and the linter has no signal that \"scheduled\" / \"completed\" / \"pending\" are meant to be members of a closed set. Typed constants paired with an `AvailableXs` collection turn the enum into a first-class type the type-checker enforces and that `Validate()` can range over. The `Available*` collection is what makes the pattern self-describing — a new contributor reads the package and immediately sees the full value space.\n23\t\n24\t#### Bad\n25\t\n26\t```go\n27\t// Untyped string constants — every call site can pass any string\n28\tconst (\n29\t\tOrderStatusPending    = \"pending\"\n30\t\tOrderStatusProcessing = \"processing\"\n31\t\tOrderStatusCompleted  = \"completed\"\n32\t\tOrderStatusFailed     = \"failed\"\n33\t)\n34\t\n35\tfunc Process(status string) error { // accepts any string; typo silent\n36\t\tif status == \"compleated\" { ... } // compiles cleanly; runtime no-op\n37\t}\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\t#### Good\n41\t\n42\t```go\n43\t// OrderStatus is the closed set of lifecycle states an Order may occupy.\n44\ttype OrderStatus string\n45\t\n46\t// Recognised OrderStatus values. The Available* collection below mirrors\n47\t// this set — keep both in sync.\n48\tconst (\n49\t\tPendingOrderStatus    OrderStatus = \"pending\"\n50\t\tProcessingOrderStatus OrderStatus = \"processing\"\n51\t\tCompletedOrderStatus  OrderStatus = \"completed\"\n52\t\tFailedOrderStatus     OrderStatus = \"failed\"\n53\t)\n54\t\n55\t// OrderStatuses is a collection of OrderStatus values, used by Validate to\n56\t// check membership in the closed set.\n57\ttype OrderStatuses []OrderStatus\n58\t\n59\t// AvailableOrderStatuses lists every OrderStatus value the system accepts.\n60\t// Validate() ranges over this collection; tests and dispatchers iterate it.\n61\tvar AvailableOrderStatuses = OrderStatuses{\n62\t\tPendingOrderStatus,\n63\t\tProcessingOrderStatus,\n64\t\tCompletedOrderStatus,\n65\t\tFailedOrderStatus,\n66\t}\n67\t\n68\t// Process advances an order to the next state for the given status.\n69\t// Returns an error if the status is unknown or the order is in a terminal state.\n70\tfunc Process(ctx context.Context, status OrderStatus) error { // type-checked at call site\n71\t\t// ...\n72\t}\n73\t```\n74\t\n75\t### Minimal Complete Implementation\n76\t\n77\t```go\n78\tpackage order\n79\t\n80\timport (\n81\t\t\"context\"\n82\t\n83\t\t\"github.com/bborbe/collection\"\n84\t\t\"github.com/bborbe/errors\"\n85\t\t\"github.com/bborbe/validation\"\n86\t)\n87\t\n88\t// 1. Define constants with explicit typing\n89\tconst (\n90\t\tPendingOrderStatus    OrderStatus = \"pending\"\n91\t\tProcessingOrderStatus OrderStatus = \"processing\"\n92\t\tCompletedOrderStatus  OrderStatus = \"completed\"\n93\t\tFailedOrderStatus     OrderStatus = \"failed\"\n94\t)\n95\t\n96\t// 2. Declare available values collection\n97\tvar AvailableOrderStatuses = OrderStatuses{\n98\t\tPendingOrderStatus,\n99\t\tProcessingOrderStatus,\n100\t\tCompletedOrderStatus,\n101\t\tFailedOrderStatus,\n102\t}\n103\t\n104\t// 3. Define singular type\n105\ttype OrderStatus string\n106\t\n107\t// 4. Implement String() method\n108\tfunc (o OrderStatus) String() string {\n109\t\treturn string(o)\n110\t}\n111\t\n112\t// 5. Implement Validate() method checking against Available* collection\n113\tfunc (o OrderStatus) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n114\t\tif AvailableOrderStatuses.Contains(o) == false {\n115\t\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, validation.Error, \"unknown order status '%s'\", o)\n116\t\t}\n117\t\treturn nil\n118\t}\n119\t\n120\t// 6. Define plural collection type\n121\ttype OrderStatuses []OrderStatus\n122\t\n123\t// 7. Implement Contains() method on collection\n124\tfunc (o OrderStatuses) Contains(status OrderStatus) bool {\n125\t\treturn collection.Contains(o, status)\n126\t}\n127\t```\n128\t\n129\t### RULE go-enum-type/validate-against-available-collection (MUST)\n130\t\n131\t**Owner**: go-architecture-assistant\n132\t**Applies when**: a Go enum type's `Validate(ctx context.Context) error` method validates against an inline switch / hardcoded value list / regex, instead of `AvailableXs.Contains(value)`.\n133\t**Enforcement**: `rules/go/validate-against-available-collection.yml` (mechanical first-pass flags `Validate` methods containing an inline `switch`) + judgment-tier LLM adjudication to confirm the switch is enum-membership validation and the package has an `Available*` collection.\n134\t**Why**: Validating against an inline switch duplicates the enum's value space — adding a new enum constant requires updating both `const (...)` and the `Validate()` body, and the type checker can't enforce the pair. Range-over-`AvailableXs` collapses the two into one declaration: the collection IS the validation source, so the only place to add a value is the collection literal. Adds-a-constant-but-forgets-to-update-validate becomes structurally impossible.\n135\t\n136\t#### Bad\n137\t\n138\t```go\n139\tfunc (o OrderStatus) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n140\t\tswitch o {\n141\t\tcase PendingOrderStatus, ProcessingOrderStatus,\n142\t\t     CompletedOrderStatus, FailedOrderStatus:\n143\t\t\treturn nil\n144\t\tdefault:\n145\t\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, validation.Error, \"unknown order status '%s'\", o)\n146\t\t}\n147\t\t// Adding a new status to const(...) without updating this switch\n148\t\t// produces silent validation failures.\n149\t}\n150\t```\n151\t\n152\t#### Good\n153\t\n154\t```go\n155\tfunc (o OrderStatus) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n156\t\tif !AvailableOrderStatuses.Contains(o) {\n157\t\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, validation.Error, \"unknown order status '%s'\", o)\n158\t\t}\n159\t\treturn nil\n160\t}\n161\t// Adding a new status to AvailableOrderStatuses automatically extends\n162\t// the validation surface. Single source of truth.\n163\t```\n164\t\n165\t## Implementation Checklist\n166\t\n167\tWhen creating a new enum type, ensure you have:\n168\t\n169\t- [ ] Constants with explicit type annotation (`const Name Type = \"value\"`)\n170\t- [ ] `var AvailableXs` collection containing all valid values\n171\t- [ ] Singular type definition (`type Status string`)\n172\t- [ ] `String() string` method on singular type\n173\t- [ ] `Validate(ctx context.Context) error` method checking against `AvailableXs`\n174\t- [ ] Plural collection type (`type Statuses []Status`)\n175\t- [ ] `Contains()` method on plural type using `github.com/bborbe/collection`\n176\t\n177\t## Usage Example\n178\t\n179\t```go\n180\ttype Order struct {\n181\t\tID       string      `json:\"id\"`\n182\t\tStatus   OrderStatus `json:\"status\"`\n183\t\tPriority Priority    `json:\"priority\"`\n184\t}\n185\t\n186\tfunc (o Order) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n187\t\treturn validation.Validate(ctx, o.Status, o.Priority)\n188\t}\n189\t\n190\tfunc ProcessOrder(ctx context.Context, order Order) error {\n191\t\t// Type-safe comparison\n192\t\tif order.Status == CompletedOrderStatus {\n193\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"order already completed\")\n194\t\t}\n195\t\n196\t\t// Collection operations\n197\t\tactiveStatuses := OrderStatuses{PendingOrderStatus, ProcessingOrderStatus}\n198\t\tif !activeStatuses.Contains(order.Status) {\n199\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"order not in active state\")\n200\t\t}\n201\t\n202\t\t// String conversion\n203\t\tfmt.Printf(\"Processing order with status: %s\\n\", order.Status.String())\n204\t\n205\t\t// Validation\n206\t\tif err := order.Validate(ctx); err != nil {\n207\t\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, err, \"invalid order\")\n208\t\t}\n209\t\n210\t\treturn nil\n211\t}\n212\t```\n213\t\n214\tSee [Go Validation Framework Guide](go-validation-framework-guide.md) for validation patterns.\n215\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"call_f957bd7b98874acf90e3a141","session_id":"698f096f-a0f8-4a78-ad91-b84cbf89d0ae","uuid":"91177718-63f4-4fe7-958f-0bb7e67d89e9","timestamp":"2026-08-14T23:24:44.860Z","subagent_type":"dark-factory:prompt-creator","task_description":"Generate prompts from spec"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014hjLtRGtc6rgPUtbEmcerv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Prompt Writing Guide\n2\t\n3\tA prompt is a markdown file that tells the YOLO agent what to build. Each prompt produces one atomic, verifiable change.\n4\t\n5\t## When to Write a Prompt\n6\t\n7\t**This document does NOT decide direct vs prompt vs spec.** That decision lives in [../choosing-a-flow.md](../choosing-a-flow.md) — the single source of truth. Read it first to confirm a prompt is the right flow.\n8\t\n9\tIn short: write a prompt when the change is a code change that does NOT carry a business-level \"why\" that deserves its own document. Markdown / docs / configs / small operator scripts → direct edits, not prompts. Features with a real business-why → spec first. See the canonical decision doc.\n10\t\n11\tYou may also write prompts manually for finer control over decomposition from an approved spec (the daemon does this automatically by default).\n12\t\n13\t## Creating a Prompt\n14\t\n15\tUse the Claude Code command:\n16\t\n17\t```\n18\t/dark-factory:create-prompt\n19\t```\n20\t\n21\tOr create manually. Location depends on status and project config:\n22\t\n23\t| Status | Directory | Purpose |\n24\t|--------|-----------|---------|\n25\t| `idea` | `prompts/ideas/` | Rough concepts, not ready for approval |\n26\t| `draft` | inbox dir (default: `prompts/`) | Complete prompts, ready for review and approval |\n27\t\n28\tThe inbox directory is configurable per project. Run `dark-factory config` to check the actual `inboxDir` path before writing prompts.\n29\t\n30\t```bash\n31\t# Check your project's inbox directory\n32\tdark-factory config | grep inboxDir\n33\t\n34\t# Idea — park it for later\n35\ttouch prompts/ideas/my-change.md\n36\t\n37\t# Draft — ready for review (use the inboxDir from config)\n38\ttouch prompts/my-change.md\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\tUse lowercase-kebab-case. Dark-factory assigns execution numbers on approve (e.g., `248-spec-044-model.md`).\n42\t\n43\t**Ordering prefix for dependent prompts.** When multiple prompts must execute in strict order, prefix with a simple number to define the sequence: `1-spec-044-model.md`, `2-spec-044-executor-timeout.md`, `3-spec-044-processor-retry.md`. Approve them in order — dark-factory replaces the prefix with execution numbers. Do NOT number standalone prompts.\n44\t\n45\t## Prompt Structure\n46\t\n47\t### Frontmatter\n48\t\n49\t```yaml\n50\t---\n51\tstatus: draft\n52\t---\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\tWhen linking to a spec:\n56\t\n57\t```yaml\n58\t---\n59\tspec: [\"030-foo-bar-baz\"]\n60\tstatus: draft\n61\tcreated: \"2026-03-11T10:00:00Z\"\n62\t---\n63\t```\n64\t\n65\t- `spec` must be a YAML array of strings — single or multiple entries\n66\t- **Canonical form is the full slug** (e.g. `[\"030-foo-bar-baz\"]`), not the bare number. Daemon-generated prompts use this form, and `pkg/slugmigrator` rewrites bare numbers to full slugs after each generation cycle so all prompts converge on the canonical form\n67\t- Bare numbers (`spec: [\"030\"]`) are accepted as input — the slug migrator resolves them to the full form at the next daemon iteration\n68\t- Only use `spec`, `status`, `created`, `issue` — dark-factory adds the rest\n69\t- Valid inbox statuses: `idea` (rough concept, needs refinement) or `draft` (complete, ready for approval)\n70\t\n71\t### Body\n72\t\n73\t```xml\n74\t<summary>\n75\t5-10 bullet points describing WHAT this prompt achieves, not HOW.\n76\tWritten for human review — no file paths, no struct names.\n77\t\n78\tGOOD:\n79\t- Projects can configure a validation command for all prompts\n80\t- The command's exit code determines success or failure\n81\t- Existing prompts continue to work unchanged\n82\t\n83\tBAD:\n84\t- Adds `validationCommand` field to `Config` in `pkg/config/config.go`\n85\t- Calls `exec.Command` in `pkg/executor/executor.go`\n86\t</summary>\n87\t\n88\t<objective>\n89\tWhat to build and why (1-3 sentences). State the end state, not the steps.\n90\t</objective>\n91\t\n92\t<context>\n93\tRead CLAUDE.md for project conventions.\n94\tRead `pkg/config/config.go` — find the `Config` struct and `Validate` method.\n95\t</context>\n96\t\n97\t<requirements>\n98\t1. Specific, numbered, unambiguous steps\n99\t2. Include exact file paths (repo-relative, not absolute)\n100\t3. Include function/type names as anchors (not line numbers)\n101\t4. Show old → new code patterns where helpful\n102\t</requirements>\n103\t\n104\t<constraints>\n105\t- Do NOT commit — dark-factory handles git\n106\t- Existing tests must still pass\n107\t- [Copy constraints from spec — agent has no memory between prompts]\n108\t</constraints>\n109\t\n110\t<verification>\n111\tRun `make precommit` -- must pass.\n112\t</verification>\n113\t```\n114\t\n115\t## `<verification>` is container-only — never operator-side\n116\t\n117\tThe prompt's `<verification>` block runs INSIDE the YOLO container. The container has: the mounted repo, `make`, language runtimes, filesystem tools (`find`, `grep`), and network read (via `tinyproxy`) for its own remote.\n118\t\n119\tThe container does NOT have: a Docker socket, the `dark-factory` CLI, cluster credentials, `kubectl*` wrappers, host scripts that shell out to Docker or the network (`scripts/*.sh` in most bborbe projects), write access to any remote other than the project's own, and — if `.dark-factory.yaml` sets `hideGit: true` or `workflow: worktree` — a working `.git` directory.\n120\t\n121\t**Container-executable commands (safe in `<verification>`):**\n122\t\n123\t- `make precommit`, `make test`, `make check`, `make ensure`\n124\t- `grep`, `find`, `ls`, `cat` — filesystem checks for expected side-effects\n125\t- Language-specific test runners (`go test ./...`, `pytest`, `pnpm test`) that don't shell out to Docker\n126\t- The change's own binary / CLI if it was built in `<requirements>`\n127\t\n128\t**Operator-executable commands (NEVER in `<verification>`):**\n129\t\n130\t- `docker` (no socket), `make build` / `make build-multiarch` / `make buca` (shell out to Docker)\n131\t- `dark-factory <cmd>` (binary not in container PATH)\n132\t- `kubectl*`, `kubectlquant*`, `kubectlprod*`, `kubectldev*` (no cluster creds)\n133\t- `scripts/*.sh` when the script requires Docker or the network beyond `tinyproxy`\n134\t- `gh pr create`, `gh release`, `gh api` (no PAT with write scope inside container)\n135\t- `git ` (bare — trailing space avoids `github`/`git@`) when `hideGit: true` or `workflow: worktree` masks `.git` — the command dies with `fatal: not a git repository`\n136\t\n137\t**Why this matters — the false-positive-pass failure mode:** the daemon's executor does NOT check `<verification>` command exit codes for failure. A `git` command that dies with `fatal: not a git repository`, or a `docker` command that dies with `command not found`, still ships. The prompt lands, the code is committed, the verification never actually ran. This is the highest-signal reason to keep operator-only commands out of prompts.\n138\t\n139\t**If the check IS operator-only** (image build, dev cluster deploy, dark-factory healthcheck, admin gateway probe), move it to the spec's `# Verification` § \"Operator-executable\" rung — see `docs/rules/spec-writing.md` § \"Verification: container-executable vs operator-executable\". Never embed operator-only commands in the prompt's `<verification>` block or inside a labelled `NOTE FOR OPERATOR` sub-block in `<constraints>` — the auditor rejects both patterns as Critical.\n140\t\n141\t**If the container really can't verify anything meaningful for this change** — the change is likely spec-scale (its verification is E2E), and the flow decision should be revisited per `../choosing-a-flow.md`.\n142\t\n143\t## Writing Rules\n144\t\n145\t### Specificity over brevity — but pick the right *kind* of specificity\n146\t\n147\tLonger, more specific prompts succeed more often *when the specificity is about contracts and anchors*, not when it's about pre-deciding every line of code the agent will write. Spelling out every if/else and every error message ships the author's bugs verbatim and prevents the agent from applying existing project conventions. See [Detail Levels](#detail-levels) below for how to pick the right grain.\n148\t\n149\tAlways include:\n150\t- Exact file paths (repo-relative)\n151\t- Function/type names as primary anchors\n152\t- Library import paths\n153\t- References to existing files demonstrating each pattern the new code follows\n154\t\n155\tOptional (depends on detail level):\n156\t- Line numbers only as hints (`~line 176`) — go stale quickly\n157\t- Old → new code patterns for find-and-replace — useful for mechanical refactors, harmful for novel work\n158\t- Inlined code bodies — see Detail Levels for when this helps vs. hurts\n159\t\n160\t### Detail Levels\n161\t\n162\tPrompts span a spectrum from \"fully scripted\" to \"intent only.\" Each level has real trade-offs. Pick deliberately; default to **Level 3 (Medium)** for most work in a mature codebase. Mismatched grain is the most common cause of prompt failure: too much detail ships author bugs, too little produces wrong abstractions.\n163\t\n164\t| Level | Shape | Typical line count |\n165\t|-------|-------|-------------------|\n166\t| 1 — Very Detailed | Full inlined function bodies, every `if`/`else` written, error messages pre-decided | 500–1500 |\n167\t| 2 — Detailed | Function signatures + key error paths inlined; agent fills bodies | 200–500 |\n168\t| 3 — Medium *(default)* | Interfaces + sequence + pattern references + failure modes; no function bodies | 80–200 |\n169\t| 4 — Soft | End-state contracts + pattern references; agent chooses structure | 40–100 |\n170\t| 5 — Very Rough | One-paragraph intent + verification command | 10–40 |\n171\t\n172\t#### Level 1 — Very Detailed (scripted)\n173\t\n174\t**Shape:** Full Go source inlined for every function. Every conditional written out. Test cases enumerated by literal value.\n175\t\n176\t**Pros:**\n177\t- Maximum reproducibility across model versions; works on cheap/weak models.\n178\t- The exact output is in the prompt — no agent interpretation needed.\n179\t- Good for teaching examples or documenting a canonical implementation.\n180\t\n181\t**Cons:**\n182\t- The author's bugs ship verbatim (the agent faithfully implements the wrong `fmt.Errorf` instead of the project's `errors.Wrapf`).\n183\t- Drifts from project style because the prompt-author writes from memory, not from existing code.\n184\t- Audit becomes line-by-line code review of the prompt — slow and error-prone.\n185\t- The agent's pattern-matching strength is wasted.\n186\t- Any reusable knowledge inside the prompt rots and re-rots across every future prompt that needs the same pattern.\n187\t\n188\t**When to use:**\n189\t- Brand-new pattern with no existing exemplar in the codebase.\n190\t- Absolute first prompt of a new project (no convention to follow yet).\n191\t- Reproducing a published reference implementation that should match an external source line-for-line.\n192\t\n193\t#### Level 2 — Detailed (annotated skeleton)\n194\t\n195\t**Shape:** Function signatures and key error paths spelled out. Imports listed. Bodies left to the agent with hints (`// retry once on transient errors per DB#8`).\n196\t\n197\t**Pros:**\n198\t- Still very prescriptive about the contract surface.\n199\t- Manageable to audit (200–500 lines, not 1500).\n200\t- Less prone to style drift than Level 1 because bodies follow project conventions.\n201\t\n202\t**Cons:**\n203\t- The author still owns most of the logic; bugs in the spelled-out error paths still ship.\n204\t- The skeleton can subtly bias the agent away from a cleaner structure it would have chosen.\n205\t\n206\t**When to use:**\n207\t- Novel logic in a familiar project (the pattern doesn't exist yet, but the style does).\n208\t- Complex algorithms where the exact shape of the control flow matters.\n209\t- Migrating off a stable external API where the call sequence is fixed.\n210\t\n211\t#### Level 3 — Medium (contracts + references) — **default**\n212\t\n213\t**Shape:** Interface signatures, sequence of operations, pattern references (`see pkg/github/client.go for the errors.Wrapf style`), explicit failure modes copied from the spec, test scenarios named (`table-test the verdict × autoApprove matrix`). No function bodies. No pre-written error messages.\n214\t\n215\t**Pros:**\n216\t- Best for translation work — applying an existing project pattern to a new feature.\n217\t- Leverages the agent's pattern-matching strength: it reads the referenced file and adopts the style automatically.\n218\t- Small enough to audit at a contract level instead of a line-by-line review.\n219\t- Prompts age well — the contract changes rarely, the implementation can be regenerated.\n220\t- Author bugs in inlined code can't ship because there is no inlined code.\n221\t\n222\t**Cons:**\n223\t- Requires at least one good in-tree exemplar for each pattern referenced.\n224\t- Weaker models may produce non-uniform code across siblings without explicit shape.\n225\t- Reviewer must trust the contract rather than the implementation.\n226\t\n227\t**When to use:**\n228\t- Most refactors and feature work in mature codebases (the default).\n229\t- Multi-prompt specs where consistency across siblings matters.\n230\t- Any case where existing code already demonstrates the conventions the new code should follow.\n231\t\n232\t#### Level 4 — Soft (constraints + goals)\n233\t\n234\t**Shape:** What must be true at the end, plus references and constraints. No interfaces enumerated. Agent decides sequence, structure, and decomposition within the file.\n235\t\n236\t**Pros:**\n237\t- Maximum agent agency — it can refactor adjacent code if that produces a cleaner result.\n238\t- Very small prompts; fast to write.\n239\t- The agent may discover a better abstraction than the author would have specified.\n240\t\n241\t**Cons:**\n242\t- Outcomes more variable across runs and model versions.\n243\t- Relies on agent capability and self-discipline; weaker models drift.\n244\t- Harder to review (less to compare against).\n245\t- Multi-prompt coordination breaks down — siblings may make incompatible decisions.\n246\t\n247\t**When to use:**\n248\t- Greenfield work with a capable model.\n249\t- Rapid iteration when the right shape is uncertain.\n250\t- Cleanup tasks where the agent is expected to use judgment about how far to refactor.\n251\t\n252\t#### Level 5 — Very Rough (intent)\n253\t\n254\t**Shape:** One paragraph stating intent. A single verification command. No constraints, no references.\n255\t\n256\t**Pros:**\n257\t- Trivial to write — minutes from idea to attempt.\n258\t- Forces the agent to do the design thinking.\n259\t- Useful as a \"first draft\" that can be refined into Level 3.\n260\t\n261\t**Cons:**\n262\t- Highly variable output; often produces wrong abstractions on first try.\n263\t- Usually needs multiple rounds with edits — total wall time is often longer than starting at Level 3.\n264\t- Almost never satisfies the audit DoD on first pass.\n265\t- No reproducibility — same prompt twice produces different code.\n266\t\n267\t**When to use:**\n268\t- Spike / exploration where the answer isn't yet known.\n269\t- Throwaway scripts.\n270\t- Rapidly evolving requirements where any specification will be out of date by the time it's written.\n271\t\n272\t### Choosing a level\n273\t\n274\t**The Level 3 default assumes documented patterns actually exist in the codebase.** In a messy or greenfield codebase with no stable exemplars, Level 3 is dishonest — the prompt promises \"see X for the style\" but X doesn't exist, and the agent silently slides back into inlining. If patterns aren't there yet, Level 2 (spelled-out signatures, hinted bodies) is more honest until a pattern is proven, then promote to Level 3.\n275\t\n276\t**Step 0 — Discover existing patterns before writing.** Spend ~2 minutes running searches like:\n277\t\n278\t```bash\n279\t# Error-wrapping style in this project\n280\trg -l 'errors\\.Wrapf|fmt\\.Errorf' pkg/ internal/ | head\n281\t\n282\t# HTTP client construction\n283\trg -l 'http\\.Client|net/http' pkg/ | head\n284\t\n285\t# Counterfeiter mock pattern\n286\trg -l 'counterfeiter:generate' pkg/ | head\n287\t\n288\t# Test framework\n289\trg -l 'ginkgo\\.|gomega\\.|testing\\.T' pkg/ | head\n290\t```\n291\t\n292\tIf a surface returns 5+ matches with a consistent pattern → Level 3 references work. If it returns 0–2 → that pattern is novel for this codebase; choose Level 2 or document the new convention in a project doc first.\n293\t\n294\t**Then ask in order:**\n295\t\n296\t1. **Did Step 0 find ≥5 matches with a consistent pattern for the surface you're touching?**\n297\t   - Yes → continue to question 2.\n298\t   - No → **patterns are missing or inconsistent.** Continue to question 4 (do NOT silently fall through to Level 3 — that's the trap).\n299\t2. **Is this a published external API that must match line-for-line?**\n300\t   - Yes → Level 1 (and link the external source).\n301\t   - No → **Level 3** (reference the exemplars from Step 0; don't re-inline). This is the common case for translation work in mature codebases.\n302\t3. *(unreachable from question 1; kept for symmetry with prior versions)*\n303\t4. **(Patterns missing.) Will the agent need to invent a novel structure?**\n304\t   - Yes → Level 4 or 5 (let it explore), then promote to Level 3 once the pattern is proven AND document it in `project/docs/`.\n305\t   - No (translation work but no exemplar yet) → **Level 2** (spelled-out signatures, hinted bodies) — more honest than fake Level 3 references that don't exist. Promote to Level 3 in a future prompt once the pattern is documented.\n306\t\n307\t**The \"fall-through-to-Level-3\" trap:** the most common mistake. Author runs no searches, picks Level 3 because it's the \"default\", references files that don't exist or don't demonstrate the pattern claimed. Agent silently inlines (because it has nothing to reference), and the prompt produces author-style code anyway — back to the original problem. Step 0 + question 1's hard split prevents this.\n308\t\n309\t### What the spectrum does NOT solve\n310\t\n311\tPattern-anchoring (Level 3) solves **convention-drift bugs** — the agent adopts the project's existing style automatically. It does **not** solve **logic bugs in genuinely novel code**. Examples that no exemplar would catch:\n312\t\n313\t- `io.LimitReader(resp.Body, 500)` placed BEFORE `json.Unmarshal` — truncates the body before parsing, but no existing file demonstrates the right ordering because no existing file has this exact concern.\n314\t- A retry classifier that maps \"POST returned 200 but review absent in GET\" to the wrong error class — the logic itself is wrong, not the style.\n315\t- An off-by-one in pagination, an inverted boolean, a swapped argument order.\n316\t\n317\tThese need **test cases the agent must satisfy** (spec acceptance criteria, failure-modes table, scenario tests) or **adversarial review** — they cannot be prevented by referring to exemplars. The detail spectrum reduces *one class* of bug; the other class needs spec-level rigor.\n318\t\n319\t### Worked example: bot-identity self-check at three levels\n320\t\n321\tSame surface (verify the GitHub bot account matches `BOT_GITHUB_LOGIN` before posting), three grain levels:\n322\t\n323\t**Level 1 — Very Detailed**\n324\t\n325\t```text\n326\t## Step 4 — Implement BotIdentityCheck\n327\t\n328\tCreate function `func CheckBotIdentity(ctx context.Context, client HTTPClient, expectedLogin string) error`:\n329\t\n330\t1. Build request:\n331\t   req, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, \"GET\", \"https://api.github.com/user\", nil)\n332\t   if err != nil {\n333\t       return fmt.Errorf(\"build request: %w\", err)\n334\t   }\n335\t   req.Header.Set(\"Authorization\", \"token \"+token)\n336\t   req.Header.Set(\"Accept\", \"application/vnd.github+json\")\n337\t\n338\t2. Execute request:\n339\t   resp, err := client.Do(req)\n340\t   if err != nil {\n341\t       return fmt.Errorf(\"execute request: %w\", err)\n342\t   }\n343\t   defer resp.Body.Close()\n344\t\n345\t[... 60 more lines of fully-written Go ...]\n346\t```\n347\t\n348\t**Cost:** The author wrote `fmt.Errorf` from memory. The project uses `errors.Wrapf(ctx, err, ...)`. The agent will faithfully ship `fmt.Errorf` — every test passes; `make precommit` lint fails or worse, the convention drifts silently.\n349\t\n350\t**Level 3 — Medium (default)**\n351\t\n352\t```text\n353\t## Bot identity check (DB#4 in spec 027)\n354\t\n355\tContract:\n356\t- Function: CheckBotIdentity(ctx, client HTTPClient, expectedLogin string) error\n357\t- Returns nil iff GitHub's GET /user response's `login` field equals expectedLogin\n358\t- Returns errors.Wrapf(ctx, ErrBotIdentityMismatch, \"got %q, want %q\", actual, expectedLogin) on mismatch\n359\t- Returns wrapped error on HTTP / parse failure per the retry-policy classification\n360\t\n361\tPattern references (read before writing):\n362\t- `pkg/github/client.go` for HTTP+errors.Wrapf style and Authorization header construction\n363\t- `pkg/githubauth/types.go` for the HTTPClient interface shape and Counterfeiter annotation\n364\t- `pkg/verdict.go` for sentinel-error pattern (see `ErrUnknownVerdict`)\n365\t\n366\tTest (use DescribeTable):\n367\t- happy: 200 + login=expectedLogin → nil\n368\t- mismatch: 200 + login=other → wrapped ErrBotIdentityMismatch\n369\t- transient: network error → wrapped per pkg/github/client.go classification\n370\t- malformed: non-JSON response → wrapped, treated as permanent\n371\t```\n372\t\n373\t**Cost:** None of the inlined bugs are possible — the agent reads `pkg/github/client.go` and adopts the existing `errors.Wrapf` style, header construction, and error classification. ~25 lines of prompt vs ~70.\n374\t\n375\t**Level 5 — Very Rough**\n376\t\n377\t```text\n378\t## Bot identity check\n379\t\n380\tThe agent self-checks that the GitHub PAT belongs to `my-bot-account`\n381\tbefore posting any review. Mismatch → refuse + diagnostic.\n382\t\n383\tverification: make precommit\n384\t```\n385\t\n386\t**Cost:** The agent might invent the function in the wrong package, miss the retry-policy integration, or choose a different error sentinel. Works for spike/exploration; needs rewrite for production.\n387\t\n388\tWhen in doubt, write **Level 3** and let audit suggest tightening to Level 2 if the agent would be likely to drift.\n389\t\n390\t### State the why, not just the what\n391\t\n392\t`<objective>` should answer *why this change matters* in one clause, not just what to build. Models reason better when they know the purpose — same effort, fewer wrong abstractions.\n393\t\n394\t```\n395\tBAD:  Add a validation command field to project config.\n396\tGOOD: Add a validation command field to project config so projects can enforce repo-specific checks (e.g. `make precommit`) before a prompt is marked complete.\n397\t```\n398\t\n399\tThe \"why\" also constrains the agent: if it considers a shortcut that breaks the stated purpose, it has a reason to reject it.\n400\t\n401\t### Title comes from the first body H1\n402\t\n403\tThe commit subject for a prompt is derived from the first markdown `# ` heading in\n404\tthe body (falling back to the filename when there is none). `#`-prefixed lines\n405\tinside fenced code blocks (`` ``` ```` or `~~~`) are ignored, so a YAML or shell\n406\tcomment quoted in a code example will never become the commit subject. To control\n407\tthe commit subject explicitly, put a real `# Your Title` line at the very top of the\n408\tbody, above any fenced block.\n409\t\n410\t### Anchor by name, not line number\n411\t\n412\tLine numbers go stale when prior prompts edit the same file:\n413\t\n414\t```\n415\tBAD:  \"At line 231, wrap the error\"\n416\tGOOD: \"In ProcessQueue, after the autoSetQueuedStatus call, wrap the error\"\n417\t```\n418\t\n419\t### Copy constraints from spec\n420\t\n421\tThe agent has no memory between prompts. Every prompt must repeat the relevant constraints from the spec.\n422\t\n423\t### One concern per prompt\n424\t\n425\tSmaller prompts succeed more often. Group coupled behaviors together, but don't mix unrelated changes.\n426\t\n427\t### Repo-relative paths only\n428\t\n429\tDark-factory runs prompts in a Docker container. Never use absolute paths (`/Users/...`) or home-relative paths (`~/...`).\n430\t\n431\t**Exception**: Files under the mounted claude config use the in-container path `/home/node/.claude/plugins/...`, NOT the host path `~/.claude-yolo/plugins/...`. See [yolo-container-setup.md](../yolo-container-setup.md#project-workspace-mount).\n432\t\n433\t### Cross-repo references\n434\t\n435\tIf a prompt needs to reference code in a sibling repo (e.g., a library at `~/Documents/workspaces/time/`), the file is NOT accessible inside the container. Options:\n436\t\n437\t1. **Point to the vendored copy** — `vendor/github.com/bborbe/time/time_parse-time.go` (preferred when the dep is already vendored).\n438\t2. **Add an `extraMount`** in `.dark-factory.yaml` to mount the sibling repo.\n439\t3. **Inline the relevant snippet** in the prompt body.\n440\t\n441\tNever leave a host-absolute path hoping the agent will figure it out — the agent will get a \"file not found\" and fail.\n442\t\n443\t### Test the boundaries the new code crosses\n444\t\n445\tA broad class of bug: new code passes every unit test because the tests verify the **shape** of what was added, but fails at runtime because it crosses a **boundary** (library validator, parser, dispatch registry, serialization, subprocess, external service) and that boundary imposes a constraint the shape tests never exercise.\n446\t\n447\t**Rule:** For every boundary the new code crosses, at least one test must traverse that boundary with the new value. Ask: *what happens to this value after it leaves the code I just wrote?*\n448\t\n449\t**Root cause framing:** the problem is *missing integration tests* — tests that exercise the same code path production traffic takes. The \"validator test\" pattern below is a cheap, mechanically-enforceable subset.\n450\t\n451\t**Common boundaries and their contracts:**\n452\t\n453\t- **Library validators / parsers** — `Validate()`, `Parse()`, `Check()`, `MustX()` enforce regex / schema / range / format at runtime. The Go compiler does not check the underlying string.\n454\t- **Registries and dispatch tables** — adding a handler / operation / route is not enough; the dispatcher must find it via the production lookup path.\n455\t- **Serialization round-trips** — JSON / YAML / protobuf tags, nested types, zero-value handling. A round-trip test is the contract.\n456\t- **External service contracts** — Kafka operation names, Prometheus label regex, DNS labels, URL schemes, HTTP route patterns.\n457\t- **Subprocess interfaces** — argv, env vars, stdin/stdout shape, exit codes. A wrong flag name silently fails.\n458\t- **Build-time constraints** — build tags, `go:generate` directives, struct tags read by code generators.\n459\t\n460\t**Two acceptable levels:**\n461\t\n462\t1. **Unit-level contract test** (cheap; use this when a single-function validator/parser exists): call the boundary function directly on the new value — `Validate(ctx)`, `Parse(...)`, marshal+unmarshal. For grouped values of the same library type, a table test enumerating all values in the package is the canonical shape.\n463\t\n464\t   ```go\n465\t   // IMPORTANT: every new <TypeName> below MUST also appear in the Validate-all\n466\t   // test table in <matching_test.go>. The boundary contract is only enforced\n467\t   // at runtime — the test is the only pre-deploy guard.\n468\t   ```\n469\t\n470\t2. **Integration test through the real boundary** (more thorough; use this when no single-function validator exists, or when the change introduces a new integration seam — publish path, registry entry, new CLI flag): drive the new value through the real production path in a test harness.\n471\t\n472\t**Scope note:** E2E deployment verification (dev deploy + smoke tests) is a spec/scenario concern, not a prompt concern. Prompts cover unit and integration; specs cover acceptance; scenarios cover end-to-end.\n473\t\n474\t**What does NOT satisfy this rule:**\n475\t\n476\t- Struct equality tests (`Expect(cmd.TaskIdentifier).To(Equal(\"foo\"))`)\n477\t- Accessor-default tests (`Expect(fm.TriggerCount()).To(Equal(0))`)\n478\t- Constant-value tests (`Expect(string(OpX)).To(Equal(\"op-x\"))`) — these assert what you typed, not what the boundary accepts.\n479\t\n480\t**Why this rule exists:** in a real incident, `IncrementFrontmatterCommandOperation = \"increment_frontmatter\"` (underscores) passed every unit test but was rejected at runtime by the cqrs validator's regex `^[a-z][a-z-]*$`, causing a silent message-retry loop in dev that took a deploy cycle to diagnose. A six-line table test calling `.Validate(ctx)` on each declared operation would have caught it at `make precommit`.\n481\t\n482\t### Never run `go mod vendor` in a prompt\n483\t\n484\t`vendor/` is a build-time artifact: gitignored, regenerated by `make buca` before `docker build`. `make precommit`'s `ensure` target wipes it and uses `-mod=mod`. Dark-factory doesn't build Docker images, so nothing it does needs `vendor/`.\n485\t\n486\t**Never write `-mod=vendor` in a verification command.** bborbe repos don't commit `vendor/`, so `go test -mod=vendor …` (and `go tool cover` on its profile) fails with \"inconsistent vendoring\". Always use `-mod=mod` for `go test` / coverage verification commands.\n487\t\n488\tDep bump: `go get foo@v1.2.3 && go mod tidy` — that's it.\n489\t\n490\tException: if the repo commits `vendor/`, document that deviation in the prompt.\n491\t\n492\t### Add imports before tidying\n493\t\n494\t`go mod tidy` (and `make ensure`, which calls it) removes any direct entry from `go.mod` that no code currently imports. A prompt step that does `go get foo@v1.2.3 && go mod tidy` BEFORE the file that imports `foo` is written will silently demote/remove the dep — the next `make precommit` then fails on the unresolved import.\n495\t\n496\t```\n497\tBAD:  Step 1: go get foo && go mod tidy\n498\t      Step 2: write code that imports foo\n499\t      → Step 1 left go.mod unchanged because nothing imported foo yet\n500\tGOOD: Step 1: write code that imports foo\n501\t      Step 2: go mod tidy (promotes foo to a direct dep)\n502\t```\n503\t\n504\tFor prompts that add a new dependency: state the order explicitly — write the import first, then tidy.\n505\t\n506\t### `make ensure` vs `make precommit` mid-implementation\n507\t\n508\tWhen a prompt says \"add a new dependency\" or \"regenerate vendored code\", the intermediate state cannot pass `make precommit` (test+check fail until the code is updated). Use `make ensure` for dependency-only preparation steps, then reserve `make precommit` for final verification.\n509\t\n510\t```\n511\tBAD: Step 1: \"Run `make precommit` to add the dep.\"   # fails — tests reference code not yet written\n512\tGOOD: Step 1: \"Run `make ensure` to add the dep.\"     # deps only\n513\t      Step N (last): \"Run `make precommit`.\"          # full check at the end\n514\t```\n515\t\n516\t## Decomposition\n517\t\n518\tWhen breaking a spec into multiple prompts:\n519\t\n520\t**The grouping test:** \"Can these behaviors be verified independently?\" If not, group them.\n521\t\n522\t**The verifiability test:** \"Can I write a test that distinguishes before vs after?\" If not, merge or sharpen.\n523\t\n524\t**Sequencing:** Most foundational prompt first. Each prompt's postconditions become the next prompt's preconditions.\n525\t\n526\t| Feature size | Typical prompts |\n527\t|--------------|----------------|\n528\t| Config change | 1 |\n529\t| Single feature | 2-3 |\n530\t| Major feature | 4-6 |\n531\t| Full project bootstrap | 8-15 |\n532\t\n533\t## Prompt Definition of Done\n534\t\n535\tA prompt is ready for approval when ALL checks pass:\n536\t\n537\t**Structure:**\n538\t- [ ] All sections present: `<summary>`, `<objective>`, `<context>`, `<requirements>`, `<constraints>`, `<verification>`\n539\t- [ ] `<summary>` has 5-10 plain-language bullets (no file paths or jargon)\n540\t- [ ] `<objective>` states end state in 1-3 sentences\n541\t- [ ] `<requirements>` are numbered, specific, unambiguous\n542\t- [ ] `<constraints>` copied from spec\n543\t- [ ] `<verification>` has a runnable command\n544\t\n545\t**Accuracy:**\n546\t- [ ] File paths exist and are correct\n547\t- [ ] Function names and signatures match current code\n548\t- [ ] No stale references to renamed/moved/deleted code\n549\t- [ ] All paths are repo-relative\n550\t\n551\t**Quality:**\n552\t- [ ] Independently verifiable\n553\t- [ ] Libraries specified with import paths\n554\t- [ ] No duplicates with completed prompts\n555\t- [ ] Every boundary the new code crosses (library validator, parser, registry, serialization, subprocess, external service) has a test that traverses it with the new value (see \"Test the boundaries the new code crosses\" above)\n556\t\n557\t## Audit and Approve\n558\t\n559\tAlways audit before approving:\n560\t\n561\t```\n562\t/dark-factory:audit-prompt prompts/my-change.md\n563\t```\n564\t\n565\tThen approve via CLI (never manually edit frontmatter):\n566\t\n567\t```bash\n568\tdark-factory prompt approve my-change\n569\t```\n570\t\n571\tThis moves the prompt from `prompts/` to `prompts/in-progress/`, assigns a number, and sets `status: queued`.\n572\t\n573\t**Direct workflow:** Can approve multiple prompts at once — they execute sequentially on the same branch.\n574\t\n575\t**PR workflow:** Approve one at a time — each depends on the previous being merged.\n576\t\n577\t## Prompt Status Lifecycle\n578\t\n579\t| Status | Directory | Meaning | How it happens |\n580\t|--------|-----------|---------|----------------|\n581\t| `idea` | `prompts/ideas/` | Rough concept, needs refinement | Human creates file |\n582\t| `draft` | `prompts/` | Complete, ready for review and approval | Human/AI creates file |\n583\t| `approved` | `prompts/in-progress/` | Queued for execution | `dark-factory prompt approve` |\n584\t| `executing` | `prompts/in-progress/` | YOLO container running | Auto (dark-factory) |\n585\t| `committing` | `prompts/in-progress/` | Container succeeded, git commit pending | Auto (dark-factory) |\n586\t| `completed` | `prompts/completed/` | Done, archived | Auto (dark-factory) |\n587\t| `failed` | `prompts/in-progress/` | Needs fix or retry | Auto (dark-factory) |\n588\t| `cancelled` | `prompts/in-progress/` | Pulled before/during execution | `dark-factory prompt cancel <id>` |\n589\t\n590\tCompleted prompts are immutable. If behavior changes later, create a new prompt.\n591\t\n592\t### Fixing an Approved Prompt\n593\t\n594\tTo edit a prompt that has already been approved (or is executing) without recreating it:\n595\t\n596\t```bash\n597\tdark-factory prompt cancel <id>     # sets status: cancelled\n598\t# edit the .md file to fix issues\n599\tdark-factory prompt requeue <id>    # sets status: queued, re-enters the execution queue\n600\t```\n601\t\n602\tNote: `unapprove` does NOT work on `cancelled` prompts (error: \"only approved prompts can be unapproved\"). Use `requeue`.\n603\t\n604\tNever manually edit the `status:` frontmatter — always use the CLI commands.\n605\t\n606\t## Next Steps\n607\t\n608\t- Run the pipeline: [running.md](../running.md)\n609\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"call_f957bd7b98874acf90e3a141","session_id":"698f096f-a0f8-4a78-ad91-b84cbf89d0ae","uuid":"f7594702-f59b-4073-9221-2ed1a0a17a7f","timestamp":"2026-08-14T23:24:44.903Z","subagent_type":"dark-factory:prompt-creator","task_description":"Generate prompts from spec"}
